1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:07,260
Welcome to this deep dive. Um, today we're looking at the EU's interoperability framework and the X-Road governance experience.

2
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:14,240
Yeah. And, uh, if you're the official running digital systems in your ministry, we're exploring how to govern the reference architecture.

3
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:19,419
Right. Because if you don't govern it correctly, your systems just become, you know, siloed.

4
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:24,440
Siloed, fragmented, uh, duplicated, or just incredibly costly to change.

5
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:29,279
Exactly. So we're unpacking the three tiers of governance for the reference architecture today.

6
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:30,220
Okay, so three tiers.

7
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:37,560
Yeah. And to understand why those tiers even exist, we kinda have to look at why governance usually fails in the first place.

8
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:38,560
Right. Why does it fail?

9
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:42,599
Well, it generally fails in one of two opposite directions.

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,360
Uh, the first is a top-heavy approach.

11
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,160
Meaning the ministers are making the calls.

12
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:53,720
Yeah, exactly. Ministers are asked to decide on granular technical details that they frankly just cannot judge.

13
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:54,940
Oh, wow. Yeah.

14
00:00:54,949 --> 00:00:57,880
And because of that, decisions just stall out entirely.

15
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,540
Okay, let me challenge that for a second though.

16
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:02,950
Um, if a top-heavy approach stalls things,

17
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:05,239
shouldn't the technicians just take the lead?

18
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:09,760
Well... I mean, they're the ones who actually understand the code and how the reference architecture functions.

19
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:10,060
Right.

20
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:12,600
So why is a bottom-heavy approach bad?

21
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:16,220
Because technicians lack political authority.

22
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:24,720
Ah. In a bottom-heavy scenario, a technical working group might try to solve that top-heavy stall by, you know, going rogue.

23
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:25,740
What does that look like?

24
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:31,750
Well, they might purchase an expensive, totally incompatible server setup just to keep the project moving.

25
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:34,619
Oh, and then you're accidentally locked into one supplier.

26
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:40,540
Precisely. They end up making political or financial commitments they have no authority to keep.

27
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:45,520
Right. And then, you know, those decisions inevitably get reversed once the budget is reviewed.

28
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:54,170
So if the ministers are too detached, and the tech specialists lack the authority to write the checks- Yeah... where is the middle ground?

29
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:57,000
That's where the three-tier model comes in.

30
00:01:57,080 --> 00:02:00,880
Okay. It assigns decisions to the specific body that's actually equipped to make them.

31
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:04,040
Got it. So at the top tier, you have the strategic council.

32
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:06,990
And these are what? The ministers and most senior officials.

33
00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:09,600
Yes. They provide the political authority.

34
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:15,000
They set the overall direction, and, uh, give the digital framework political cover.

35
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:16,240
Okay, so they're the muscle.

36
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:22,070
Yeah. Crucially, they remove the political obstacles that technicians just cannot touch.

37
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:28,720
Like what? Like a reluctant minister or a turf dispute between two agencies over who owns the data.

38
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:30,720
Oh, or like a massive budget fight.

39
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:35,269
Exactly. But they stay completely out of the technical weeds.

40
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:38,740
Which brings us to the middle tier, I assume.

41
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:39,480
The steering committee.

42
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:45,340
Right. This is your program leadership. So your chief digital officer and sector CIOs sit here.

43
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:46,660
And they're the ones holding the budget.

44
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,640
They hold the budget, and they steer the framework month to month.

45
00:02:49,980 --> 00:02:52,660
So, for instance, they prioritize the use case catalog.

46
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:57,100
The practical ranked list of real-world problems the architecture needs to solve.

47
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:01,600
Exactly. Which leaves the bottom tier for the technical working groups.

48
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:06,090
The specialists. Yeah. The architects and security experts doing the detailed technical work.

49
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:08,200
They define the standards and protocols.

50
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:10,100
Okay, but wait. I feel like we missed something.

51
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:11,960
Isn't there something actually running all this?

52
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:17,980
Oh, yes. The operating authority. Yeah. They just run the platform underneath all three tiers day to day.

53
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:20,430
Right. Okay. So in the real world,

54
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:26,800
um, when two security architects in a technical working group completely disagree on a standard,

55
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:29,480
who breaks the tie?

56
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:31,760
Do we have to bother the minister?

57
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:36,920
No, absolutely not. The decision escalates exactly one level up to the steering committee.

58
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:37,680
Oh, I see.

59
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:40,480
It's a shared rhythm of escalation and delegation.

60
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:43,520
Decisions are always made at the lowest possible tier.

61
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:46,720
So it only reaches the strategic council if it's political.

62
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:55,300
Right. Only if the steering committee realizes the technical dispute is actually masking a larger political conflict do they escalate it up to the ministers.

63
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:57,380
That saves the ministers scarce time,

64
00:03:58,060 --> 00:03:59,440
uh, while keeping the project moving.

65
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:05,960
Exactly. It ensures business and IT decide the same cross-cutting questions in the same room.

66
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:06,590
To summarize,

67
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:15,109
this structure provides political authority at the strategic council, program leadership at the steering committee, and technical specialization at the working groups

68
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:16,899
with an operating authority underneath.

69
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:25,560
Each has one job and decides at its level, governing without drowning ministers in detail or letting technicians make commitments they cannot keep.

70
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:26,880
Sources are in the video description.
